feat(deploy): run the backup where the deployment runs, and drop the host timer (#237)

The schedule was a host scheduler: launchd on one platform, systemd on the
other, two unit layouts, and a scheduler that has to exist at all. Three CI
failures in one day came from that split, every one of them in the harness
rather than in the thing being tested. A container runtime is already required
by everything else here, so the schedule lives where the deployment lives.

`innsegl-backup` starts and stops with the stack, runs once at start-up — a
fresh stack otherwise has no backup for a whole interval while reporting a
healthy backup service — and then on its interval. The sleep is the whole
scheduler on purpose: it is a thing anyone can read, and the CronJob that
eventually owns this schedule replaces the loop and keeps the container.

IT HOLDS NO RUNTIME SOCKET, which is the reason for all of this and not a
detail. What it replaced reached the ledger and the object store through that
socket, which is root on the host.

Running only while the stack is up makes the backup's liveness the stack's
liveness. That is a real cost and it is paid in the open: every run records its
verdict, and the healthcheck reports a missed window, a mismatch or a failure
in words. `unverified` is healthy, deliberately — the dump exists and restores,
and what was missing was sealed segments to adjudicate it against, which is the
ordinary state of a ledger whose tail has not sealed yet.

`make innsegl-backup` now runs through the service's own image, so the one-off
run is the same command on both platforms and needs nothing installed.

Measured against the deployment: the service took a backup on first start,
fetched the segments, adjudicated them OK, wrote its marker, and reported
healthy. Every verdict was then driven through the healthcheck by hand — a
missed window, a mismatch, a failure, an unreadable marker and an absent one —
and the socket assertion was mutation-tested by mounting one and watching the
gate go red.

OPS-035 is superseded in doc 07 rather than deleted: the timer it describes no
longer exists.

Driven by OPS-044, OPS-046 and OPS-047.

